    {
      "object_id": "mathia_synthesis_072351fefb5704c10c157a87c2a81a45acca8107eb855cb4a760aed2864f978a",
      "object_role": "synthesis",
      "proposed_content": "The Euler product first recodes zeta as prime-local factors; logarithms and weighted counting then convert multiplicative prime structure into additive data compatible with analytic or combinatorial operations. In Riemann's route, continuation, transform inversion, and residues separate a smooth pole contribution from zero contributions, and the later RH equivalence identifies zero location with the permitted scale of the prime-counting remainder. Tchebychev's boundary-regularity and factorial arguments are complementary consequences of related recodings, not steps that by themselves derive Riemann's explicit formula.",
