    {
      "object_id": "mathia_synthesis_827b47820a50c49bfe3fd7cbc1807dc0e42854d1ad7ff14653db5e509930f32a",
      "object_role": "synthesis",
      "proposed_content": "The source presents three logically different restrictions on a possible RH counterexample. Equality between an argument-principle total and critical-line sign changes certifies every zero only inside a finite window; fixed-boundary density estimates limit how numerous sufficiently off-line zeros can be as height grows; and positive-proportion theorems establish abundance of simple critical-line zeros from the good side. These channels constrain different domains and populations and therefore cannot be combined as a single scalar degree of confirmation.",
