    {
      "object_id": "mathia_synthesis_d23f043068fb4362aabe758ba25c65313b16b738ae26273b3c6fefbfaea02012",
      "object_role": "synthesis",
      "proposed_content": "The shared L-function package motivates a theory broader than zeta: local Euler factors, global analytic symmetry, and distinguished zero, pole, and special-value data recur across arithmetic and automorphic settings. Yet the elliptic-curve example shows that even continuation and a functional equation may depend on proving a deep modular identification, while primitive Maass forms retain an Euler product, functional equation, and expected RH without the direct arithmetic-geometric provenance used in that example. Together with the explicitly missing classical analogues of cohomology, Frobenius, and an index theorem, this makes pure importation of known finite-field geometry an unsupported assumption rather than an available solution.",
